description:
The core design principle: GenTailscale.Socket and GenTailscale.Listener must
not call ts_elixir directly. Instead, they operate against a behaviour that
abstracts the underlying I/O.

## Transport Behaviour

defmodule GenTailscale.Transport do
  @callback connect(keys_file, opts) :: {:ok, dev} | {:error, reason}
  @callback listen(dev, addr, port) :: {:ok, listener} | {:error, reason}
  @callback accept(listener) :: {:ok, stream} | {:error, reason}
  @callback recv(stream) :: {:ok, binary} | {:error, reason}
  @callback send(stream, binary) :: {:ok, non_neg_integer} | {:error, reason}
  @callback send_all(stream, binary) :: {:ok, non_neg_integer} | {:error, reason}
  @callback local_addr(stream_or_listener) :: {:ok, {ip, port}} | {:error, reason}
  @callback remote_addr(stream) :: {:ok, {ip, port}} | {:error, reason}
  # close/shutdown when ts_elixir supports them
end

## Real Implementation

defmodule GenTailscale.Transport.TsElixir do
  @behaviour GenTailscale.Transport
  # Delegates to Tailscale.connect, Tailscale.Tcp.*, etc.
end

## Deterministic Simulator

defmodule GenTailscale.Transport.Simulator do
  @behaviour GenTailscale.Transport

  Backed by an Agent or GenServer that:
  - Maintains simulated network state (connections, buffers, partitions)
  - Uses a seeded PRNG for all non-deterministic decisions
  - Injects faults according to a fault schedule:
    * Partial reads (return N < requested bytes)
    * Delayed reads (block for simulated time)
    * Connection drops (return {:error, :closed} at random points)
    * Send failures
    * Reordering (for active mode message delivery)
  - All state transitions are deterministic given the seed
  - Logs all events for replay/debugging

  Fault injection parameters (configured per test):
  - :partial_read_probability (0.0-1.0)
  - :drop_probability (0.0-1.0)  
  - :max_read_size (force small reads to exercise buffering)
  - :close_after_n_ops (simulate connection failure after N operations)
  - :seed (for deterministic replay)
end

## Why This Architecture

From sled.rs: "clear mapping from input to output" — the Socket GenServer's
state machine logic is identical whether backed by real NIFs or the simulator.
All fault injection happens at the Transport layer, not inside the GenServer.

From TigerBeetle: test safety properties (data integrity, no duplication, no loss)
under fault injection, then test liveness (does recv eventually return? does
active mode eventually deliver?) after faults are healed.
